==English==

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|floow|t=woolly substance, down, nap, lint}}, also spelt {{m|en|flough}}, {{m|en|flue}}, and {{m|en|flew}}, of {{unk|en|title=uncertain}} origin (cf. {{cog|ang|flōh|t=that which is flown off, fragment, piece}}). Possibly representing a {{blend|en|flue|puff|nocap=1}}. ''Flue'' is perhaps a borrowing of {{bor|en|vls|vluwe}} (compare {{cog|dum|vloe}}), or perhaps onomatopoeic; compare dialectal {{cog|en|floose}}, {{m|en|flooze}}, {{m|en|fleeze|t=particles of wool or cotton; fluff; loose threads or fibres}},
{{cog|da|fnug|t=down, fluff}}, {{cog|sv|fnugg|t=speck, flake}}. Alternatively, {{etyl|vls|en}} {{m|vls|vluwe}} may derive from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|velu|t=hairy, furry}}, ultimately from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|villus}} ("shaggy hair, tuft of hair"). For words of similar sound and meaning in other languages, compare {{cog|ja|フワフワ||lightly, softly|tr=fuwafuwa}}, {{cog|hu|puha}} (“soft, fluffy”), Polish {{m|pl|puchaty}} (“soft, fluffy”).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/flʌf/}}
* {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 fluff.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌf}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Anything [[light]], [[soft]] or [[fuzzy]], especially [[fur]], [[hair]], [[feather]]s.
# Anything [[inconsequential]] or [[superficial]].
#: {{ux|en|That article was basically a bunch of '''fluff'''. It didn't say anything substantive.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[lapse]] or [[mistake]], especially a [[mistake]] in an [[actor]]'s [[line]]s.
#: {{synonyms|en|flub}}
# {{lb|en|New England}} [[marshmallow creme|Marshmallow creme]].
# {{lb|en|LGBT}} A [[passive]] partner in a [[lesbian]] relationship.
# {{lb|en|Australia|euphemistic}} A [[fart]].

